<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- One key, for the person in the room.

     A message from a guest appears on the television as a card, and that card
     says "F8 to reply". This is what makes that true. Without it the card is
     an instruction to press a key that does nothing, which is worse than not
     mentioning replying at all.

     F8 rather than a letter, because Kodi's own bindings own most letters (c
     is the context menu, i is information) and taking one of those off
     somebody's remote to add a chat key is a poor trade. The function keys
     are free, and a keyboard is what this is for.

     Installed to ~/.kodi/userdata/keymaps/fourth-player.xml. Kodi merges every
     file in that directory, so this adds a key and changes nothing else. -->
<keymap>
  <global>
    <keyboard>
      <f8>RunScript(script.fourthplayer,chat)</f8>
    </keyboard>
  </global>
</keymap>
